.TH "cbor::diagnostic_notation" "3" "November 02, 2026" "mbedded-cbor 0.9.0" "mbedded-cbor"
.SH NAME
cbor::diagnostic_notation \- Return the diagnostic notation of one CBOR
data item.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]<cbor/cbor.hpp>\f[R]

\f[B]namespace\f[R] cbor {
std::expected< std::string, error > diagnostic_notation(std::string_view const encoded);
}
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Writes the item in the diagnostic notation of RFC 8949 section 8.
Heads that are not in preferred form retain an encoding indicator, and
indefinite\-length form is retained.
Tags are written by number with their content; tag content is not
interpreted and tag 29 is not resolved.
The function does not check UTF\-8 or duplicate map keys.
.SH PARAMETERS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]encoded\f[R]: The encoded item.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Its diagnostic notation, or an error.
.SH ERRORS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]too_little_data\f[R]: input ends before the item ends.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]syntax_error\f[R]: an invalid head or chunk, or bytes follow the
item.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]nesting_depth_exceeded\f[R]: the item exceeds
limits::nesting_depth.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]string_length_exceeded\f[R]: a string exceeds an active limit.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]container_elements_exceeded\f[R]: a container exceeds an active
limit.
.SH NOTES
O(n), where n is the number of input bytes.
The result uses O(n) memory and allocates.
The recursive call stack uses O(d) memory, where d is the item nesting
depth.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: a1 61 61 83 01 f9 41 00 41 01\f[R]
cbor::diagnostic_notation(bytes); \f[I]// \(dq{\(rs\(dqa\(rs\(dq: [1, 2.5, h\(aq01\(aq]}\(dq\f[R]
\f[I]// bytes: 9f 01 ff\f[R]
cbor::diagnostic_notation(bytes); \f[I]// \(dq[_ 1]\(dq\f[R]
\f[I]// bytes: 01 02\f[R]
cbor::diagnostic_notation(bytes); \f[I]// syntax_error\f[R]
.EE
